# TILING PROBLEM

PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Given a 2 x n floor and tiles of size 2 x 1, find the number of ways to completely tile the floor.

A tile can be placed in two ways:

1. Vertically - occupies 2 x 1
2. Horizontally - two tiles are required to occupy a 2 x 2 space

INTUITION:
For the last part of the floor, we have two choices:

1. VERTICAL PLACEMENT:
   Place one tile vertically.
   This occupies one column, leaving a 2 x (n-1) floor.

   Number of ways = T(n-1)

2. HORIZONTAL PLACEMENT:
   Place two tiles horizontally.
   These occupy two columns, leaving a 2 x (n-2) floor.

   Number of ways = T(n-2)

Therefore:

T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2)

BASE CASE:
T(0) = 1
T(1) = 1

n = 0 -> There is 1 way to tile an empty floor.
n = 1 -> There is only 1 way: place one tile vertically.

EXAMPLE:
For n = 5:

T(5) = T(4) + T(3)
= 5 + 3
= 8

Therefore, there are 8 ways to tile a 2 x 5 floor.

## JAVA CODE:

public class TilingProblem {

```
// Function to calculate the number of ways
// to tile a 2 x n floor using 2 x 1 tiles
public static int tilingProblem(int n) {

    // Base case:
    // If n is 0 or 1, there is only 1 way
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }

    // Case 1: Place one tile vertically.
    // Remaining floor = 2 x (n-1)
    int fnm1 = tilingProblem(n - 1);

    // Case 2: Place two tiles horizontally.
    // Remaining floor = 2 x (n-2)
    int fnm2 = tilingProblem(n - 2);

    // Total ways = vertical ways + horizontal ways
    int totalWays = fnm1 + fnm2;

    return totalWays;
}

public static void main(String args[]) {

    // Find the number of ways to tile a 2 x 5 floor
    System.out.println(tilingProblem(5));
}
```

}

## OUTPUT:

8

TIME COMPLEXITY:
O(2^n)

SPACE COMPLEXITY:
O(n)

KEY CONCEPT:
This problem follows the Recursion + Choice pattern.

At every step:

T(n)
/  
T(n-1)  T(n-2)
Vertical Horizontal

Recurrence Relation:

T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2)

This problem is similar to the Fibonacci sequence.
